@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex introduction

Whisper-dvr is an Emacs package that provides a convenient interface for
transcribing audio files from digital voice recorders (DVRs) using the
whisper.el package.  It streamlines the workflow of selecting audio files
from a DVR and inserting transcriptions directly into your current buffer.

@section Features
@cindex features

@itemize @bullet
@item
Interactive file selection with completion, displaying filename, size,
and modification time.

@item
Support for multiple audio formats including MP3, WAV, and M4A.

@item
Integration with whisper.el for high-quality transcription using
OpenAI's Whisper model.

@item
Configurable DVR directory path for different recorder setups.

@item
Safety checks to prevent accidental overwrites in read-only buffers.
@end itemize

@section Requirements
@cindex requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item
Emacs 27.1 or later

@item
whisper.el package (available from MELPA)

@item
A working Whisper installation (whisper.cpp or OpenAI Whisper)
@end itemize

@node Installation
@chapter Installation
@cindex installation

@section From MELPA
@cindex MELPA installation

Once whisper-dvr is available on MELPA, you can install it using the
following methods:

@subsection Using package.el

@lisp
M-x package-refresh-contents RET
M-x package-install RET whisper-dvr RET
@end lisp

@subsection Using use-package

@lisp
(use-package whisper-dvr
  :ensure t
  :after whisper
  :custom
  (whisper-dvr-directory "/Volumes/IC RECORDER/REC_FILE/FOLDER01"))
@end lisp

@section Manual Installation
@cindex manual installation

Clone the repository and add it to your load path:

@example
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/whisper-dvr.git ~/.emacs.d/site-lisp/whisper-dvr
@end example

Then add to your init file:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/.emacs.d/site-lisp/whisper-dvr")
(require 'whisper-dvr)
@end lisp

@section Installing the Info Manual
@cindex info manual installation

To install the Info manual for access within Emacs:

@example
cd /path/to/whisper-dvr
make info
sudo make install-info
@end example

Alternatively, install to a user directory:

@example
make install-info PREFIX=~/.local
@end example

Then add the directory to @code{Info-additional-directory-list}:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'Info-additional-directory-list "~/.local/share/info")
@end lisp

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration

Whisper-dvr provides several customization options accessible through
the Emacs customization interface or by setting variables directly.

@section Customization Group
@cindex customization group

All whisper-dvr options are in the @code{whisper-dvr} customization group:

@lisp
M-x customize-group RET whisper-dvr RET
@end lisp

@section Configuration Variables

@defopt whisper-dvr-directory
@vindex whisper-dvr-directory
The directory path containing audio files from the digital voice recorder.
This should point to the folder where your DVR stores recordings.

Default value: @code{"/Volumes/IC RECORDER/REC_FILE/FOLDER01"}

Example configuration for different operating systems:

@lisp
;; macOS with Sony IC Recorder
(setq whisper-dvr-directory "/Volumes/IC RECORDER/REC_FILE/FOLDER01")

;; Linux with mounted USB device
(setq whisper-dvr-directory "/media/user/IC_RECORDER/VOICE")

;; Windows
(setq whisper-dvr-directory "E:/REC_FILE/FOLDER01")
@end lisp
@end defopt

@defopt whisper-dvr-file-extensions
@vindex whisper-dvr-file-extensions
A list of audio file extensions to include when listing files from the
DVR directory.

Default value: @code{("mp3" "wav" "m4a")}

To add support for additional formats:

@lisp
(setq whisper-dvr-file-extensions '("mp3" "wav" "m4a" "ogg" "flac"))
@end lisp
@end defopt

@node Usage
@chapter Usage
@cindex usage

This chapter describes the typical workflow for using whisper-dvr to
transcribe audio files from your digital voice recorder.

@section Basic Workflow
@cindex basic workflow

@enumerate
@item
Connect your digital voice recorder to your computer and ensure it is
mounted.

@item
Open or create a buffer where you want the transcription to appear.
Position the cursor where you want the text inserted.

@item
Run @kbd{M-x whisper-dvr}.

@item
Select an audio file from the completion list.  The list shows the
filename, file size, and modification date for each recording.

@item
Wait for the transcription to complete.  The text will be inserted at
point in your buffer.
@end enumerate

@section Example Session
@cindex example session

@example
;; Open a new org-mode file for meeting notes
C-x C-f ~/notes/meeting-2024-01-15.org RET

;; Position cursor where transcription should appear
;; Then invoke whisper-dvr
M-x whisper-dvr RET

;; Select from the completion list:
;; recording001.mp3    2.1M  2024-01-15 10:30
;; recording002.mp3    1.8M  2024-01-15 14:45
;; ...

;; Choose the desired file and press RET
;; Transcription is inserted at point
@end example

@section Setting the DVR Directory
@cindex setting directory

If your DVR uses a different mount point or folder structure, you can
change the directory interactively:

@example
M-x whisper-dvr-set-directory RET
Set DVR directory: /media/user/RECORDER/VOICE RET
@end example

This change persists for the current Emacs session.  To make it
permanent, add the configuration to your init file.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands
@cindex commands

This chapter documents all interactive commands provided by whisper-dvr.

@deffn Command whisper-dvr
@findex whisper-dvr
List audio files from the configured DVR directory and transcribe the
selected file using whisper.el.  The transcription is inserted at point
in the current buffer.

This command performs the following checks before proceeding:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Verifies that the current buffer is not read-only.

@item
Warns if the current buffer is not visiting a file (prompts for
confirmation to continue).

@item
Verifies that the DVR directory exists and contains audio files.
@end itemize

The completion interface displays each file with its name, human-readable
size, and modification timestamp.

Keybinding suggestion:

@lisp
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c w d") 'whisper-dvr)
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn Command whisper-dvr-set-directory dir
@findex whisper-dvr-set-directory
Interactively set the DVR directory to @var{dir}.  When called
interactively, prompts for the directory path with completion.

This is useful when you have multiple recording devices or when the
mount point changes between sessions.

@lisp
;; Called programmatically:
(whisper-dvr-set-directory "/path/to/new/dvr")
@end lisp
@end deffn

@node Internal Functions
@chapter Internal Functions
@cindex internal functions

This chapter documents the internal functions used by whisper-dvr.
These functions are not intended for direct use but are documented here
for developers who wish to extend or modify the package.

@defun whisper-dvr--list-audio-files
@findex whisper-dvr--list-audio-files
Return a list of audio files in @code{whisper-dvr-directory}.  Files are
filtered by the extensions specified in @code{whisper-dvr-file-extensions}.

Signals a @code{user-error} if the directory does not exist.

Returns a list of absolute file paths.
@end defun

@defun whisper-dvr--format-file-entry filepath
@findex whisper-dvr--format-file-entry
Format @var{filepath} for display in the completion list.  Returns a
string containing the filename (without directory), human-readable file
size, and modification timestamp.

The format is designed to align columns for easy scanning:

@example
filename.mp3                              2.1M  2024-01-15 10:30
@end example
@end defun

@node Testing
@chapter Testing
@cindex testing

Whisper-dvr includes a comprehensive test suite using ERT (Emacs Lisp
Regression Testing).  This chapter describes how to run the tests and
the structure of the test suite.

@section Running Tests
@cindex running tests

@subsection From the Command Line

Use the provided Makefile to run all tests:

@example
cd /path/to/whisper-dvr
make test
@end example

To run tests with verbose output:

@example
make test-verbose
@end example

@subsection From Within Emacs

Load the test file and run ERT:

@lisp
(load-file "whisper-dvr-test.el")
M-x ert RET t RET
@end lisp

To run a specific test:

@lisp
M-x ert RET whisper-dvr-test-default-directory RET
@end lisp

@section Test Categories
@cindex test categories

The test suite is organized into several categories:

@table @strong
@item Unit Tests: Custom Variables
Tests that verify the default values of customization options.

@item Unit Tests: whisper-dvr--list-audio-files
Tests for the file listing function, including error handling for
non-existent directories and extension filtering.

@item Unit Tests: whisper-dvr--format-file-entry
Tests for the file entry formatting function, verifying correct display
of filenames, sizes, and timestamps.

@item Unit Tests: whisper-dvr-set-directory
Tests for the directory setting command.

@item Unit Tests: whisper-dvr
Tests for the main interactive command, including buffer state checks.

@item Integration Tests
End-to-end tests that verify the complete workflow from file selection
to transcription invocation.

@item Edge Case Tests
Tests for unusual inputs such as special characters in filenames, very
large files, and zero-size files.
@end table

@section Test Helpers
@cindex test helpers

The test file provides several helper functions and variables for mocking:

@defvar whisper-dvr-test--mock-files
Mock file list for testing.
@end defvar

@defvar whisper-dvr-test--mock-attrs
Mock file attributes for testing.
@end defvar

@defvar whisper-dvr-test--whisper-file-called
Boolean flag tracking whether @code{whisper-file} was called.
@end defvar

@defvar whisper-dvr-test--whisper-file-arg
Stores the argument passed to @code{whisper-file}.
@end defvar

@defun whisper-dvr-test--reset-state
Reset all test state variables to their initial values.
@end defun

@defun whisper-dvr-test--make-mock-attrs size mtime
Create mock file attributes with the given @var{size} (in bytes) and
@var{mtime} (modification time).
@end defun

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting
@cindex troubleshooting

This chapter addresses common issues and their solutions.

@section DVR Directory Not Found
@cindex directory not found

@strong{Error:} @code{DVR directory does not exist: /Volumes/IC RECORDER/...}

@strong{Solutions:}

@enumerate
@item
Ensure your digital voice recorder is connected and mounted.

@item
Check the mount point on your system.  On macOS, use Finder or
@code{ls /Volumes/}.  On Linux, check @code{/media/} or @code{/mnt/}.

@item
Update @code{whisper-dvr-directory} to match your device's mount point:

@lisp
M-x whisper-dvr-set-directory RET /correct/path RET
@end lisp
@end enumerate

@section No Audio Files Found
@cindex no audio files

@strong{Error:} @code{No audio files found in ...}

@strong{Solutions:}

@enumerate
@item
Verify that the directory contains audio files with supported extensions.

@item
Check that your DVR stores files in a supported format (MP3, WAV, M4A).

@item
Add additional extensions if your DVR uses a different format:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'whisper-dvr-file-extensions "wma")
@end lisp
@end enumerate

@section Buffer is Read-Only
@cindex read-only buffer

@strong{Error:} @code{Current buffer is read-only; cannot insert transcription}

@strong{Solution:}

Switch to a writable buffer before running @code{whisper-dvr}.  If you
need to make the current buffer writable:

@lisp
M-x read-only-mode RET
@end lisp

@section Whisper Transcription Fails
@cindex transcription failure

If transcription fails after file selection, the issue is likely with
the whisper.el configuration.  Verify that:

@enumerate
@item
whisper.el is properly installed and configured.

@item
The Whisper backend (whisper.cpp or OpenAI API) is accessible.

@item
The audio file is not corrupted.
@end enumerate

Consult the whisper.el documentation for backend-specific troubleshooting.

